+---
+name: setup-ai-apollo-declarative-backend
+description: "Configure or resume backend AI providers, OAuth or key references, application instructions, policies, preprocessing and controlled tools."
+---
+
+# Setup Backend AI
+
+Use the active backend project and its own `scripts/setup_project.py`; read `ai_development/backend_setup.md` first. Receive the confirmed project identity, environment and shared configuration from root setup, or inspect them when invoked directly. Reuse confirmed choices, ask only for missing decisions, and preserve deferred/declined choices unless the developer changes them. Do not rerun unrelated root or feature setup.
+
+Inspect `ai_development/project_setup.json`, relevant nonsecret configuration and existing feature records. Keep the CLI's `managed_environment`, `planned_integrations`, secret references and completion fields intact. After an authorized setup apply, merge only this feature's entry under `feature_setup` using the record contract in backend_setup.md. Preview-only work must not write the record. Return actual configuration, verification and pending actions to root setup; never equate configuration with deployment or live acceptance.
+
+Read ai_development/backend_ai_spec.md before configuring MODEL_PROVIDER=disabled/ollama/openai_compatible/openai_oauth and WORKFLOW_ENGINE=disabled/langgraph. Collect endpoint, generation/embedding model names, external runtime credential references and bounded execution limits. Keep providers disabled until accepted; apply supported settings through preview/apply and verify capabilities separately with scripts/verify_ai_provider.py. --plan records intent without activation.
+
+For LLM setup or behavioral changes, read ai_development/backend_ai_setup.md. Reuse the project's existing choices before asking for new ones. Cover provider/generation/embedding models, runtime secret references and limits, the trusted system message sent alongside the user message, ordered preprocessors/enrichment, injection/output policy, and authorized tools/tool chains. Record project-specific behavioral choices and their planned/implemented/verified status in ai_development/ai_setup_choices.md; this is an operator record, not runtime configuration.
+
+Configure the developer-approved system message as a separate trusted message alongside the user message, never as text prepended into the user message. This Codex setup skill guides the operator and is not model input. Trace the current system message to TRUSTED_INSTRUCTIONS/enrich() in ai_workflow.py, policies to ai_policy.py and the AIPolicy port, preprocessing/enrichment to graph nodes/context port, and tools to AIToolExecutor plus their independently authorized owning services. Keep all actual composition in app.py. The system message is currently code-owned and there is no generic processor/tool-chain setting today; identify requested extensions as implementation work and never invent CLI/environment options. Preserve the original user input and treat user/retrieved/tool text as untrusted context. Heuristic injection checks cannot grant access or replace tool permissions.
+
+Apply supported settings through the CLI. Configuration-only setup records unsupported customizations as planned. When their implementation is requested, include the corresponding code/wiring and policy/permission/lifecycle tests before reporting them active. Verify instructions/message roles, processor order, denied/invalid tools, output-policy rejection, timeout/cancellation and real-provider generation/tool/embedding behavior as applicable. Report configuration written separately from composed behavior and observed acceptance. Read the runbook for exact current capabilities, examples and update preservation.
+
+For Responses OAuth, follow backend_ai_setup.md: external account-specific session directory, shipped connect/session commands, writable UID10001 renewal storage and independent embedding settings. Never reuse API-key mounts for OAuth or infer embedding access from the OAuth grant. Keep live acceptance and deployment separate.
+
+Embedding deferral: AI_EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=disabled supports generation/tools without embedding credentials or calls. Hosted OpenAI embeddings remain a later optional setup step. Existing same mode retains backward compatibility; disabling embeddings never disables generation or silently substitutes a local model.
+
+
+## Provider access and verification
+
+
+Use the selected project's provider adapter and collect only nonsecret choices: endpoint reachable from its runtime, generation model with required tool support, supported authentication method, external credential/session reference, time/output/tool-call limits and intended local/server runtime. Reuse existing project choices. For API-key access, the operator provisions the key outside the checkout/image and the skill configures only its path and read-only runtime mount. For a supported OAuth adapter, use the shipped connection/status/refresh/sign-out workflow and dedicated renewable session directory described in backend_ai_setup.md. Do not assume an arbitrary provider supports OAuth or that generation authorization grants embeddings. Check host permissions and single refresh ownership before container/server activation.
+
+Preserve explicit embedding deferral; delegate structured memory configuration to setup-memory-apollo-declarative-backend. Do not enable or disable memory as a side effect of provider setup. Development fixtures prove interfaces, storage, authorization and failure paths; they do not prove live credentials or semantic retrieval quality. Do not require real accounts, API keys, local model downloads or embedding calls merely to complete framework tests. Delegate document knowledge setup to setup-knowledge-apollo-declarative-backend; enabling generation or pgvector does not configure knowledge retrieval. When a derived project later enables embeddings, collect that provider/model/endpoint and separate external credential reference, index/version compatibility and retrieval acceptance requirements through setup.
+
+Before declaring LLM access ready, distinguish offline validation from an explicitly requested live generation/tool probe and actual container/server reachability. Report deferred checks. Include the project's system message, policy/preprocessing, authorized tools and optional memory configuration from the linked runbooks; configuring access does not automatically implement custom behavioral extensions. Never bundle a developer's test session or credentials into a derived project.
